  interf:["Rheumatoid factor and heterophile antibodies → falsely high","Very high triglycerides or bilirubin → assay-dependent error","Assays are not harmonised — results in DDU and FEU differ roughly two-fold. Always check which your lab reports."],
  pearls:["D-dimer excludes, it does not diagnose. Use it only in low or intermediate pre-test probability (Wells, Geneva). In high probability, image regardless of the result.","<cite index=\"53-1\">D-dimer rises physiologically through pregnancy and in the third trimester exceeds the conventional 500 µg/L cutoff in almost all women</cite> — a standard cutoff is therefore useless near term.","The pregnancy trimester ranges here describe what is physiologically expected — they are NOT an exclusion threshold. Published trimester intervals differ several-fold between assays and populations, so a value inside this range does not rule out VTE in pregnancy. Suspected PE or DVT in pregnancy goes down an imaging pathway, not a D-dimer.","Age-adjusted cutoff (age × 10 ng/mL FEU above 50 years) safely increases specificity without losing sensitivity.","Check whether your lab reports FEU (fibrinogen equivalent units) or DDU — mixing them up doubles or halves the apparent result."],
  rel:["ptinr","aptt","pct"],
  src:["Age-adjusted D-dimer cut-off (age x 10 ng/mL FEU over 50 years) — ADJUST-PE study","ISTH overt DIC scoring system"]
