  dec:["Dengue, malaria, scrub typhus, sepsis — the leading causes in Indian practice","Immune thrombocytopenia (ITP)","DIC, TTP, HUS","Hypersplenism, portal hypertension, chronic liver disease","Drugs — heparin (HIT), quinine, vancomycin, linezolid, valproate, cotrimoxazole","Bone marrow failure or infiltration, aplastic anaemia","B12/folate deficiency","Gestational thrombocytopenia, pre-eclampsia, HELLP"],
  interf:["EDTA-dependent platelet clumping → pseudothrombocytopenia. Confirm on the smear and repeat in a citrate tube (multiply result by 1.1).","Giant platelets counted as red cells → falsely low","Clotted sample → falsely low","Red cell or leucocyte fragments → falsely high"],
  pearls:["Always look at the smear before acting on a low count — pseudothrombocytopenia is common and leads to unnecessary transfusion.","Spontaneous bleeding is uncommon above 20,000/µL in a stable patient; transfusion thresholds differ by setting (10,000 prophylactic, 50,000 for surgery, 100,000 for neuraxial or CNS procedures).","A falling platelet count with a rising haematocrit in a febrile Indian patient is dengue with plasma leak until proven otherwise.","Never transfuse platelets in TTP or HIT — it worsens thrombosis."],
