  dec:["GI losses — vomiting, diarrhoea, fistula","Diuretics (loop and thiazide)","Hyperaldosteronism, Cushing syndrome, liquorice","Renal tubular acidosis types 1 and 2","Insulin, beta-agonists (salbutamol nebulisation), alkalosis — all shift potassium into cells","Magnesium depletion — potassium will not correct until magnesium is replaced","Refeeding syndrome"],
  interf:["Haemolysis → falsely high (the single commonest lab artefact)","EDTA tube drawn before the chemistry tube → grossly high potassium with low calcium","Delayed separation or refrigeration of whole blood → falsely high","Marked thrombocytosis → serum potassium high, plasma normal"],
  pearls:["A high potassium in a well patient with no ECG change is haemolysis until proven otherwise — repeat before treating.","Get an ECG immediately for any potassium ≥6.5: peaked T waves, flat P waves, wide QRS. Calcium gluconate first to protect the myocardium, then insulin-dextrose and salbutamol to shift, then definitive removal.","Always check and replace magnesium in refractory hypokalaemia.","EDTA carryover gives a pathognomonic picture: very high K with very low Ca — recollect in correct order of draw."],
