  interf:["Jaffe method: bilirubin falsely lowers, while glucose, ketones, cephalosporins and ascorbate falsely raise. Enzymatic methods are more specific.","Haemolysis and lipaemia affect Jaffe methods"],
  pearls:["Report eGFR with every creatinine. The current standard is <cite index=\"25-1\">CKD-EPI 2021: eGFR = 142 × min(Scr/κ,1)^α × max(Scr/κ,1)^−1.200 × 0.9938^age × 1.012 if female, where κ = 0.7 for females and 0.9 for males</cite>; <cite index=\"30-1\">α is −0.241 for females and −0.302 for males</cite>.","The 2021 equation is race-free — <cite index=\"26-1\">the National Kidney Foundation recommends laboratories report eGFR using the CKD-EPI equation without the race variable</cite>. Do not use the old race coefficient.","Creatinine lags GFR by 24–48 h. In early AKI the trend matters more than the absolute value.","Muscle mass is the hidden variable: a creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL means very different things in a 90 kg labourer and a frail 80-year-old."],
  rel:["urea","potassium","urineacr","cystatin"],
  src:["CKD-EPI 2021 creatinine equation (race-free)","KDIGO 2012 AKI creatinine criteria and CKD GFR categories"]
