  dec:["Insulin or sulfonylurea excess — the commonest cause","Prolonged fasting, malnutrition, alcohol binge","Adrenal insufficiency, hypopituitarism","Severe liver failure, advanced CKD","Insulinoma, non-islet cell tumour hypoglycaemia","Sepsis in infants and the elderly","Delayed separation in a plain tube (artefactual)"],
  interf:["Plain or EDTA tube with delayed separation → falsely low glucose","High-dose ascorbic acid → interference on some glucometers","Haematocrit extremes affect point-of-care meters","Paracetamol and dopamine interfere with some POC strips"],
  pearls:["ADA criteria for diabetes: fasting ≥126 mg/dL, 2 h OGTT ≥200 mg/dL, HbA1c ≥6.5%, or random ≥200 with classic symptoms. Confirm with a repeat test unless symptoms are unequivocal.","India uses the single-step DIPSI test for GDM — 75 g glucose regardless of fasting state, 2 h value ≥140 mg/dL diagnoses GDM. It is endorsed by MoHFW because it needs no fasting visit, but validation studies in Indian women show low sensitivity against WHO 1999 and IADPSG criteria, so a normal DIPSI does not exclude GDM where suspicion is high.","The fasting alternative is IADPSG/WHO 2013: 75 g OGTT with fasting ≥92, 1 h ≥180, or 2 h ≥153 mg/dL — any one value diagnoses GDM.","Never diagnose diabetes on a glucometer reading — it needs a venous laboratory sample.","Always check a fingerprick glucose in any patient with altered sensorium. Hypoglycaemia mimics stroke and is reversible."],
  rel:["hba1c","ketones","sodium"],
  src:["ADA Standards of Care diagnostic thresholds for diabetes and prediabetes","WHO/IDF diagnostic criteria for diabetes mellitus"]
