    interf:["Haemolysis has little effect, but severe lipaemia interferes with some enzymatic methods."],
    pearls:["BUN and urea are NOT the same number. Urea (mg/dL) = BUN (mg/dL) × 2.14. Indian labs usually report 'Blood Urea' (range about 15–45 mg/dL); US labs report BUN (about 7–20 mg/dL). Reading one against the other's range is a common and avoidable error — check the heading on the report.","Urea:creatinine ratio helps localise the problem. A disproportionately high urea (ratio >20:1 using mg/dL BUN:creatinine) suggests a pre-renal cause or GI bleed rather than intrinsic renal disease.","Urea is a poorer marker of GFR than creatinine because diet, catabolism and hydration all move it independently.","A normal urea does not exclude significant renal impairment — it rises late."],
    rel:["creatinine","sodium","potassium","urineacr"],
    src:["Pathology Harmony (UK) agreed adult and paediatric biochemistry reference intervals"]
