    pearls:["Blood β-hydroxybutyrate is the right test; urine ketone dipsticks measure acetoacetate and can look paradoxically worse during recovery as β-hydroxybutyrate converts to acetoacetate. Do not use urine ketones to judge whether DKA is improving.","Euglycaemic DKA on SGLT2 inhibitors is easily missed — check ketones in any unwell patient on these drugs even if glucose is normal.","Resolution of DKA is defined by ketones below 0.6 mmol/L and a normal bicarbonate and pH, not by glucose alone.","Children with type 1 diabetes and any intercurrent illness should have ketones checked during sick-day rules, not just glucose."],
