    dec:["Iron, B12 or folate deficiency before treatment","Aplastic anaemia and marrow infiltration","Chronic kidney disease (erythropoietin deficiency)","Anaemia of chronic disease","Pure red cell aplasia, including parvovirus B19 in haemolytic disease"],
    interf:["Howell-Jolly bodies, malarial parasites and nucleated red cells can be miscounted as reticulocytes by some automated methods."],
    pearls:["The reticulocyte count answers one question: is the marrow responding? A high count in anaemia means loss or destruction; a low count means production failure. Divide every anaemia this way first.","The raw percentage misleads in severe anaemia because the denominator is small. Use the absolute count (×10⁹/L), or the corrected count = retic% × (patient Hct ÷ normal Hct).","A reticulocyte response 5–10 days after starting haematinics confirms both the diagnosis and adherence. No response means wrong diagnosis, wrong dose, not taking it, or a second deficiency.","A sudden fall in reticulocytes in a patient with chronic haemolysis suggests parvovirus B19 aplastic crisis — a paediatric emergency in sickle cell disease and hereditary spherocytosis."],
    rel:["hb","ldh","haptoglobin","bilirubin","ferritin"],
