    critical:{ txt:"Absolute neutrophil count <500/µL (0.5 ×10⁹/L) — severe neutropenia; neutropenic sepsis risk", lo:500, hi:null, analyte:["neut"] },
    inc:["Neutrophilia: bacterial infection, steroids, stress, trauma, surgery, smoking, G-CSF, myeloproliferative disease","Lymphocytosis: viral infection, pertussis, tuberculosis, CLL (in older adults, a persistent lymphocytosis needs flow cytometry)","Monocytosis: tuberculosis, chronic infection, recovery phase of infection, CMML","Eosinophilia: parasitic infestation (very common in India — hookworm, ascaris, filaria, strongyloides), allergy, asthma, drug reaction, eosinophilic disorders","Basophilia: chronic myeloid leukaemia — uncommon enough that it should prompt a look for CML"],
    dec:["Neutropenia: chemotherapy, antithyroid and antiepileptic drugs, viral infection, overwhelming sepsis, B12/folate deficiency, hypersplenism, aplastic anaemia","Lymphopenia: HIV, steroids, severe illness, tuberculosis, lymphoma, post-chemotherapy","Benign ethnic neutropenia — common in people of African ancestry and in some Middle Eastern groups; a lifelong low neutrophil count with no infection risk"],
    interf:["Clotted or aged samples give unreliable differentials.","Automated counters cannot classify blasts or atypical lymphocytes reliably — a film is needed."],
    pearls:["Act on the absolute count, never the percentage. A neutrophil 'percentage' of 40% means very different things at a total count of 2000 and 20000.","ANC below 500/µL is severe neutropenia: fever in that setting is neutropenic sepsis and needs antibiotics within the hour, before any result comes back.","Persistent eosinophilia in India is parasitic until proven otherwise — treat empirically for helminths where appropriate before an extensive workup.","A persistent lymphocytosis above about 5000/µL in an adult over 50 needs flow cytometry to exclude CLL.","Basophilia is rare; when genuine and persistent, think chronic myeloid leukaemia."],
