    critical:null,
    inc:["Infection, including tuberculosis — a classically high ESR in India","Any inflammatory or autoimmune disease: rheumatoid arthritis, SLE, vasculitis","Giant cell arteritis and polymyalgia rheumatica — ESR above 50 mm/h is part of the classification criteria","Multiple myeloma and other paraproteinaemias (rouleaux formation)","Malignancy, chronic kidney disease, anaemia, pregnancy, old age, obesity"],
    dec:["Polycythaemia and severe leucocytosis","Sickle cell disease and spherocytosis (abnormally shaped cells do not stack)","Hypofibrinogenaemia and severe liver disease","Congestive heart failure; high-dose steroids"],
    interf:["Anaemia raises the ESR independently of inflammation — a confounder that is easy to forget.","Delay, tilting of the tube and an under-filled tube all distort the result."],
    pearls:["ESR is slow and indirect — it reflects fibrinogen and immunoglobulin driving rouleaux, so it rises over days and falls over weeks. CRP rises and falls within hours. For acute illness, CRP is the better test; for monitoring chronic inflammatory disease, ESR still has a place.","An ESR above 100 mm/h has a short differential: infection (especially TB and osteomyelitis), myeloma, malignancy, and giant cell arteritis. It deserves a definite explanation.","Interpret against age and sex. A rough guide for the upper limit is age ÷ 2 in men, and (age + 10) ÷ 2 in women.","Anaemia inflates the ESR. In a patient with a haemoglobin of 7 g/dL, a modestly raised ESR may mean nothing at all.","A normal ESR does not exclude serious disease, including giant cell arteritis — treat on clinical grounds when suspicion is high."],
    rel:["crp","hb","totalprotein","pct"],
    src:["York and Scarborough Teaching Hospitals NHS FT haematology/ESR reference ranges (BCSH-based)"]
