    tube:{ id:"edta", vol:"2 mL", handling:"Make films within 2 h of collection — EDTA storage artefact (echinocytes, vacuolated neutrophils, degenerate cells) sets in quickly and can be mistaken for disease." },
    prep:{ fasting:"Not required",
      items:["State the clinical question on the form. A film read 'blind' is far less useful than one read for a stated purpose — suspected haemolysis, malaria, leukaemia, thrombocytopenia.","For malaria, send thick AND thin films, ideally at the time of fever spike, and repeat up to three times 12–24 h apart before excluding it.","Make the films promptly; a film from an aged EDTA sample shows artefact that mimics pathology."],
      patient:["If malaria is suspected, you may be asked to give samples more than once, including when your fever rises."] },
    assayDep:false,
    units:{ conv:"Descriptive", si:"Descriptive", factor:null },
    ranges:[
      { rid:"r1", kind:"note", grp:"Normal film", sex:"any", age:"adult", preg:null, conv:{lo:null,hi:null,txt:"Normochromic normocytic red cells; normal white cell morphology and distribution; platelets adequate on film"}, si:{lo:null,hi:null,txt:"Normochromic normocytic red cells; normal white cell morphology and distribution; platelets adequate on film"} }
    ],
    critical:{ txt:"Blasts, malarial parasites, schistocytes with thrombocytopenia (?TTP), or a leukaemic picture — inform the clinician at once", lo:null, hi:null },
    inc:["Findings that change management: blasts (acute leukaemia), schistocytes (microangiopathy — TTP, HUS, DIC), malarial parasites, sickle cells, spherocytes, target cells, basophilic stippling (lead, thalassaemia), hypersegmented neutrophils (B12/folate deficiency), toxic granulation and left shift (sepsis), rouleaux (myeloma), atypical lymphocytes (viral infection)"],
    dec:["Not applicable — this is a descriptive examination."],
    interf:["Delayed film preparation from an EDTA sample creates artefact that mimics real morphological abnormality.","A thick film is more sensitive for malaria but harder to speciate; always pair it with a thin film."],
    pearls:["The film is the cheapest and often the most informative haematology test available. When the counts do not fit the clinical picture, ask for a film before ordering anything expensive.","Schistocytes with thrombocytopenia in an unwell patient is a medical emergency — think TTP and seek haematology advice the same day; it is treatable and rapidly fatal untreated.","In India a negative malaria film does not exclude malaria. Repeat films, and use a rapid antigen test alongside; low parasitaemia and partially treated infection are easily missed.","Hypersegmented neutrophils (five or more lobes) point to B12 or folate deficiency and can appear before the MCV rises.","Ask the laboratory directly when the question is specific. A telephone conversation with the haematologist about a film often settles a diagnosis faster than further tests."],
    rel:["hb","dlc","platelet","malaria","retic"],
    src:["Standard morphological practice; descriptive test with no numeric reference interval"]
