    interf:["In-vitro haemolysis from a difficult draw lowers haptoglobin and is a frequent cause of a false 'haemolysis' result.","Being an acute-phase reactant, concurrent inflammation can push a haemolysis-suppressed haptoglobin back into the normal range."],
    pearls:["An undetectable haptoglobin with a raised LDH, raised reticulocytes and raised unconjugated bilirubin is the classic haemolysis quartet. Haptoglobin is the most specific of the four for intravascular haemolysis.","The acute-phase trap matters: a septic patient who is also haemolysing may have a 'normal' haptoglobin. Interpret alongside CRP.","A low haptoglobin in isolation, with normal LDH and reticulocytes, is more likely liver disease or congenital deficiency than haemolysis.","In suspected transfusion reaction, a falling haptoglobin supports intravascular haemolysis."],
