    interf:["Under-filled citrate tube falsely lowers the result.","Very high heparin or fibrin degradation products interfere with the Clauss method."],
    pearls:["In postpartum haemorrhage, fibrinogen is the first coagulation factor to fall to critical levels. A fibrinogen below 2 g/L in PPH predicts severe haemorrhage and should trigger cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate — do not wait for it to reach the usual 1 g/L threshold used in other settings.","Remember the pregnancy baseline: a fibrinogen of 2.5 g/L is normal in a non-pregnant adult but is dangerously low at term, where 3.5–6.5 g/L is expected.","Fibrinogen is central to the DIC score alongside platelets, PT and D-dimer.","As an acute-phase protein, it can be 'normal' early in DIC because sepsis has driven it up from a high baseline. Serial values matter more than a single one."],
