    critical:{ txt:"Time-dependent inhibitor pattern with bleeding — possible acquired haemophilia; discuss with haematology urgently", lo:null, hi:null },
    inc:["Failure to correct: lupus anticoagulant, specific factor inhibitors, heparin, DOACs","Time-dependent (incubated) failure: acquired factor VIII inhibitor — acquired haemophilia A"],
    dec:["Correction indicates a factor deficiency: congenital (haemophilia A or B, factor XI, XII), liver disease, vitamin K deficiency, warfarin, or DIC"],
    interf:["Heparin or a DOAC in the sample invalidates the interpretation — always check what the patient is taking.","A poorly filled citrate tube can prolong the baseline test and send the whole workup down the wrong path."],
    pearls:["The principle is simple: mix patient plasma 1:1 with normal plasma. Normal plasma supplies roughly 50% of every factor, which is more than enough for a normal clotting time. If it corrects, the patient lacked a factor; if it does not, something in the patient's plasma is inhibiting the reaction.","Acquired haemophilia A is the diagnosis not to miss: an elderly or postpartum patient with new, often dramatic bleeding, an isolated prolonged APTT, and a mixing study that corrects immediately but prolongs again after incubation at 37 °C. It is treatable and carries high mortality if missed.","A lupus anticoagulant typically fails to correct immediately and is not time-dependent; it is associated with thrombosis, not bleeding, despite the prolonged APTT.","Always interpret with the clinical bleeding history. A prolonged APTT in a patient who has never bled, with no correction, is far more likely lupus anticoagulant than a factor inhibitor."],
    rel:["aptt","ptinr","lupusanticoag","factor8"],
    src:["Standard coagulation laboratory practice; descriptive test"]
