    inc:["Not clinically significant."],
    dec:["Inherited protein S deficiency","Pregnancy — falls markedly and normally","Oestrogen-containing contraception and hormone therapy","Warfarin and vitamin K deficiency (protein S is vitamin K-dependent)","Liver disease, acute thrombosis, DIC","Nephrotic syndrome, HIV infection, L-asparaginase"],
    interf:["Pregnancy, oestrogen and warfarin all lower protein S — between them they explain most low results, and none of them indicates inherited deficiency."],
    pearls:["Protein S has more acquired causes of a low level than almost any other thrombophilia test. Before labelling a patient deficient, exclude pregnancy, the pill, warfarin and liver disease, and repeat on a second occasion.","Free protein S antigen is generally preferred to total antigen, because only the free fraction is active.","A low protein S found during pregnancy should not be acted upon — retest at least 3 months postpartum and off hormonal contraception.","As with all thrombophilia testing, ask first whether the result will change management. In unprovoked VTE the duration of anticoagulation is usually decided clinically."],
    rel:["proteinc","antithrombin","lupusanticoag"],
    src:["Standard thrombophilia panel reference intervals with pregnancy-specific values"]
