    critical:null,
    inc:["Warfarin therapy (modest rise)","Acute hepatitis; renal transplantation"],
    dec:["Inherited antithrombin deficiency — the most thrombogenic of the inherited thrombophilias","Heparin therapy (consumption)","Acute thrombosis and DIC","Nephrotic syndrome — urinary loss","Severe liver disease (reduced synthesis)","L-asparaginase; oestrogen therapy","Neonates: physiologically low"],
    interf:["Heparin itself lowers antithrombin, so a sample taken on treatment under-reads.","Acute thrombosis consumes antithrombin, producing a transient deficiency that is not inherited."],
    pearls:["Suspect antithrombin deficiency in heparin resistance — a patient who needs unusually large doses of unfractionated heparin to reach a therapeutic APTT. Heparin works by potentiating antithrombin, so without it the drug does little.","Inherited antithrombin deficiency carries the highest thrombotic risk of the classic inherited thrombophilias and often needs lifelong anticoagulation after a first event.","As with protein C and S, timing is everything: do not test during the acute event or on heparin.","Nephrotic syndrome is an important acquired cause — antithrombin is lost in the urine, which partly explains the thrombotic tendency of that condition."],
