    inc:["Graves disease and toxic nodular goitre","T3 toxicosis — a raised FT3 with a normal FT4 and suppressed TSH, commoner in iodine-deficient areas and early Graves","Excess thyroxine or liothyronine therapy","Biotin interference (artefactual)"],
    dec:["Hypothyroidism (usually a late finding; FT4 falls first)","Non-thyroidal illness ('sick euthyroid') — low T3 is the earliest and commonest abnormality in any serious illness","Amiodarone, high-dose steroids, propranolol (impaired T4 to T3 conversion)","Starvation and severe malnutrition"],
    interf:["Biotin raises FT3 and FT4 and lowers TSH on streptavidin-biotin immunoassays.","Heterophile and anti-T3 autoantibodies cause unpredictable interference.","Non-thyroidal illness lowers FT3 without thyroid disease."],
    pearls:["Do not order FT3 routinely. TSH with FT4 answers almost every thyroid question; FT3 earns its place mainly in suspected T3 toxicosis and in monitoring treated hyperthyroidism.","A low FT3 with a normal TSH and FT4 in a sick inpatient is the sick euthyroid pattern — it reflects the illness, not thyroid disease, and should not be treated. Repeat thyroid tests after recovery.","FT3 is the least reliable thyroid test in pregnancy and in critical illness; interpret with caution in both.","If the pattern makes no clinical sense (frank biochemical thyrotoxicosis in a well patient), suspect biotin before suspecting rare disease — stop it for 72 h and repeat."],
    rel:["tsh","ft4","t3total","tpoab"],
    src:["MTW NHS Trust and Synnovis adult biochemistry reference ranges (Roche/Siemens platforms)"]
