    critical:null,
    inc:["Hashimoto thyroiditis — TPO antibodies positive in about 90%","Graves disease — positive in about 75%","Postpartum thyroiditis","Other autoimmune disease: type 1 diabetes, coeliac disease, pernicious anaemia, Addison disease","Healthy people, especially women and with age — up to 10–15% are positive without thyroid disease"],
    dec:["Not applicable."],
    interf:["Thyroglobulin antibodies interfere with thyroglobulin measurement in thyroid cancer follow-up — they must always be measured alongside it.","Biotin interference on immunoassay platforms."],
    pearls:["A positive TPO antibody does not by itself mean thyroid disease or justify treatment. It indicates autoimmunity and raises the risk of future hypothyroidism.","Its genuine clinical uses: deciding whether to treat subclinical hypothyroidism (positive antibodies favour treatment and predict progression), predicting postpartum thyroiditis, and explaining a goitre.","In pregnancy, TPO positivity with a TSH in the upper part of the range identifies women at higher risk of progression; many units monitor them each trimester.","In treated thyroid cancer, thyroglobulin antibodies must be measured with every thyroglobulin — if present, they make the thyroglobulin result unreliable as a tumour marker."],
    rel:["tsh","ft4","thyroglobulin"],
    src:["Typical manufacturer cut-offs for TPO and thyroglobulin antibody immunoassays (method-dependent)"]
