    inc:["Ovulation (the physiological mid-luteal rise)","Pregnancy","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Progesterone supplementation, including luteal support in IVF","Ovarian cysts and some ovarian tumours"],
    dec:["Anovulation — the main clinical use of a low mid-luteal value","Luteal phase deficiency","Ectopic pregnancy or failing intrauterine pregnancy (a low progesterone in early pregnancy suggests non-viability)","Hypogonadotropic states"],
    interf:["Mistimed sampling is the dominant source of error — a 'low' progesterone taken on the wrong day means nothing.","Biotin interference on immunoassay platforms."],
    pearls:["The commonest error with this test is timing. 'Day 21 progesterone' is shorthand for the mid-luteal phase in a 28-day cycle. In a woman with a 35-day cycle, day 21 is still follicular and will be low in a perfectly ovulatory woman.","A mid-luteal progesterone above 30 nmol/L confirms ovulation. Values between 15 and 30 are equivocal — repeat in the next cycle with careful timing.","In irregular cycles, serial weekly samples from mid-cycle until the next period are more informative than a single mistimed value.","In early pregnancy of uncertain location, progesterone is used alongside serial hCG: a very low progesterone suggests a failing pregnancy, though it does not localise it."],
    rel:["lh","fsh","estradiol","bhcg","amh"],
    src:["Standard mid-luteal progesterone ovulation thresholds; conversion ng/mL x 3.18 = nmol/L"]
