    critical:null,
    inc:["Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Adrenal carcinoma or adenoma — a markedly raised DHEAS points to an adrenal rather than ovarian source","Cushing syndrome (ACTH-dependent)","Premature adrenarche in children","DHEA supplements"],
    dec:["Adrenal insufficiency","Hypopituitarism","Normal ageing — DHEAS declines steadily from the third decade","Exogenous steroid suppression"],
    interf:["Age-dependence is extreme; comparing against a general adult range will mislead in the young and the old.","Biotin interference on immunoassay platforms."],
    pearls:["DHEAS is almost entirely adrenal in origin, which makes it useful for localising androgen excess: a very high DHEAS points to the adrenal, while a high testosterone with a normal DHEAS points to the ovary.","A DHEAS above about 700 µg/dL in a woman with virilisation should prompt adrenal imaging.","Mild elevations are common and non-specific in PCOS and carry no separate significance.","Because there is no diurnal rhythm and the half-life is long, DHEAS can be sampled at any time — unlike cortisol or testosterone."],
