    specimen:"Serum",
    tube:{ id:"sst", vol:"2 mL", handling:"Separate within 2 h. RETAIN a frozen aliquot — a PAIRED convalescent sample 2–4 weeks later showing a rising titre is far more informative than any single value." },
    prep:{ fasting:"Not required",
      items:["A RISING TITRE on paired samples 2–4 weeks apart is the meaningful finding. A single raised value in a population with frequent streptococcal exposure proves very little.","Request ANTI-DNase B alongside: it is more sensitive for skin infection (pyoderma-associated glomerulonephritis), where ASO is often not raised, and it rises later and stays raised longer.","Use age-matched upper limits of normal — titres are physiologically much higher in school-age children than in adults, and in populations with high streptococcal exposure.","Send with a throat swab, CRP, ESR, full blood count, urinalysis and ECG when rheumatic fever is suspected; an echocardiogram is required to detect subclinical carditis.","Note that ASO is used to establish ANTECEDENT streptococcal infection — it is not a test for current pharyngitis, for which a throat swab or rapid antigen test is appropriate."],
      patient:["This blood test looks for evidence of a recent throat or skin infection with a particular bacterium, not for an infection you have today.","A second sample two to four weeks later is often needed, because a rise between the two is what matters.","If rheumatic fever is confirmed, long-term preventive penicillin injections are essential to protect the heart — these must not be stopped without advice."] },
