    critical:{ txt:"Acute rheumatic fever with carditis — admit, treat the streptococcal infection, manage heart failure, and start secondary prophylaxis with benzathine penicillin, which must then continue for years; failure to maintain prophylaxis is the principal cause of progressive rheumatic heart disease", lo:null, hi:null },
    inc:["Recent group A streptococcal infection: pharyngitis (ASO rises in 1–3 weeks, peaks at 3–6 weeks, falls over months), pyoderma (anti-DNase B more reliable)","Acute rheumatic fever and post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis — as evidence of antecedent infection","Post-streptococcal reactive arthritis, Sydenham chorea, PANDAS (contested)","Healthy carriers and children in high-exposure populations often have raised baseline titres with no disease","Hyperlipidaemia and some liver disease can cause non-specific elevation"],
    dec:["No recent streptococcal infection","Early sampling (before the antibody has risen) — hence the need for a convalescent sample","Prompt antibiotic treatment may blunt the rise","Skin infection — ASO is frequently NOT raised, which is why anti-DNase B is added"],
    interf:["ASO alone misses about 20% of cases; combining ASO with anti-DNase B raises the detection of antecedent streptococcal infection to roughly 95%.","High background titres in endemic populations make a single value hard to interpret.","Lipaemic serum and liver disease cause non-specific elevation.","Antibiotics given early can blunt the antibody response."],
    pearls:["ASO establishes that a streptococcal infection has HAPPENED; it does not diagnose rheumatic fever. Acute rheumatic fever is diagnosed by the revised Jones criteria, with evidence of antecedent streptococcal infection as a supporting requirement, not by the titre itself.","Add ANTI-DNase B. Together the two tests detect about 95% of antecedent streptococcal infections, and anti-DNase B is essential when the preceding infection was in the skin, as in pyoderma-associated glomerulonephritis.","The paired sample is what makes the test useful: a two-fold or greater rise between acute and convalescent sera is strong evidence. A single raised titre in an Indian school-age child may simply reflect common exposure.","Rheumatic heart disease remains a major cause of acquired heart disease in India. The revised Jones criteria apply the moderate- and high-risk population thresholds here, accept monoarthritis and polyarthralgia as major criteria, and require echocardiography to detect subclinical carditis.","Secondary prophylaxis with intramuscular benzathine penicillin, continued for years according to the severity of carditis, is the single most effective intervention to prevent progressive valve damage. Counsel the family thoroughly — lapses in prophylaxis, not diagnostic failure, cause most rheumatic heart disease."],
    rel:["crp","esr","urineroutine","rf","ana","complement"],
    src:["Revised Jones criteria (2015) for acute rheumatic fever including moderate/high-risk population thresholds; age-stratified ASO and anti-DNase B upper limits of normal"]
